# OOP

Write object-oriented Java. Behavior lives on types, not loose maps, static utility bags, or global state.

- Encapsulate state. Dependents call methods; they do not touch fields or parse `Map<String, Object>`.
- New work is a class (use case, service, model, record, enum). Do not add a static helper class when a type already owns that job.
- Abstractions stay abstract: inject the interface (port / `CacheProvider` / `FileStorage`), never the implementation. Register the impl as the Spring bean behind it.
- Prefer composition over copying logic. Extend the core base types (`BaseUseCase`, `BaseController`, `AggregateRoot`, `BaseMapper`, …) instead of parallel hierarchies.
- Do not leak library types (`RedisTemplate`, `MinioClient`, `KafkaTemplate`, `HttpServletRequest`) past the owning adapter. Callers use this project's models.

```java
// ❌ BAD — loose map
Map<String, String> deviceFacts() { return Map.of("os", System.getProperty("os.name")); }

// ✅ GOOD — type owns the data
public record DeviceSnapshot(String os) { }
```
